<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

! Siege Resilience ~ how the mesh outlasts the necrospire

''PROPOSED — unbuilt design intent.'' This meme names the survival bearing for a nexus under permanent siege; it rests at Synthesis, the mechanisms named await their build (cross-check before any RULED language).

The fiction sets the test at its worst: the ''necrospire'' — a persistent adversary that pressures an isolated nexus for //five centuries//, never relenting, slowly creeping through key after key, node after node. No outside hand arrives to save the mesh; no global authority restores order. The engineering question hides inside the myth: ''how does a federation of households survive an adversary that outlasts every individual node, key, and operator?'' The answer never wins the static fight — it ''resets the clock the adversary races against''.

<<~ ahu #/two-clocks>>

!! Two Clocks ~ authority refresh ⊥ flow self-heal

The siege presses on two independent surfaces, and the mesh keeps two clocks against them — the same AUTHORITY ⊥ FLOW master cut the cap-stack rides ([[vessel-caps|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps]]):

* ''AUTHORITY clock'' — //who holds the secret and the membership//. This clock MUST tick FASTER than the adversary creeps. If the necrospire compromises shares slower than the mesh re-randomizes them, it never assembles a live threshold.
* ''FLOW clock'' — //state convergence//. This clock MUST self-heal from ANY corrupted state, with no outside hand — a corrupted replica converges back to correct purely by gossip with its honest peers.

The two never fuse. Authority answers //"can this principal still act?"// and refreshes on a schedule; flow answers //"does the state still agree?"// and repairs continuously. A design that collapses them into one clock loses the property that the FLOW layer self-heals ''even while'' the AUTHORITY layer mid-rotates.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reset-the-clock>>

!! Reset The Clock ~ the master insight

A static defence loses a 500-year siege by arithmetic: given enough time, a creeping adversary eventually holds `t` shares and breaks any fixed `t`-of-`n` threshold. The mesh refuses the static fight and ''resets the clock instead'' — it periodically re-randomizes the secret shares so that every share the adversary already stole turns worthless.

''Proactive Secret Sharing (PSS)'' carries the move: each refresh round adds a fresh random polynomial with `g(0) = 0` — the secret stays fixed, every individual share rotates. So the adversary no longer races to //eventually// collect `t` shares; it must collect `t` ''within a single refresh window'', then watch its haul expire. Past compromises compose to nothing across a refresh boundary. The 500-year clock collapses to the length of one window.

<<~moves static-threshold-defence/eventually-falls -> proactive-refresh/expires-every-theft on/g(0)=0-re-randomization if/refresh-faster-than-creep do/reset-the-window-not-win-the-fight>>

''Disambiguation — this re-key/re-share `epoch` = the liveness LEASE.'' The refresh window's
`epoch` (the AUTHORITY clock, #two-clocks — re-key / re-share, max-register, coordinator-free)
names the liveness ''lease'' (it bounds //who may still act//), DISTINCT from the CRDT-history
''compaction''-epoch (the snapshot-restart that bounds //state-history growth//) at
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/compaction-handlers">>. Same word, two mechanisms — they
MAY co-occur at a boundary, never fuse. ''Both ride ONE mechanism'' — a monotone frontier that only rolls
forward — in two carriers: an integer max-register where the writer-set is fixed, a hash-linked chain head
where the writer-set itself rotates. A grant binds to the frontier and goes stale when it moves past:
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/epoch-binding-surfaces">>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cadence>>

!! Cadence ~ the refresh floor + the triggers

The refresh fires on a ''scheduled FLOOR plus event-triggers'', never on a global clock:

* a ''24-hour scheduled floor'' — a guaranteed minimum refresh, even under no observed attack.
* ''event-triggers that may only SHORTEN the window'', never lengthen it: a membership change (a raised [[kapae|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae]] on the membership stack), quorum re-availability after a partition heals, or an IDS / algedonic alarm (#/self-stabilization). A trigger brings the next refresh forward; nothing pushes it back.

The refresh itself runs ''CHURP-style'' — cheap dynamic-committee handoff, so the committee may change shape between rounds without a costly full re-deal. The schedule floor stays ''LOCAL per committee'' — no global tick, FFZ-aligned (no global now; each committee keeps its own clock and event-triggers, see [[causal-islands|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands]]).

''Tuning the window `T`.'' Set the floor against measured detect-and-evict time, never a guessed constant: `T ≤ (t − margin) / λ / k`, where `λ` measures the adversary's per-node creep-rate, `k ≈ 4` carries a safety factor, and `margin` reserves headroom below the threshold `t`. Shorten `T` as measured `λ` rises.

''The bigger risk than cadence: CORRELATED compromise.'' A short window protects nothing if one exploit falls every node at once — a shared config makes `λ` a single shared event, not `n` independent ones. So the load-bearing defence diversifies node configurations to keep `λ` ''per-node-independent''; the refresh cadence only buys time //given// that independence holds. Monoculture defeats the clock-reset before it starts.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-active-prober>>

!! The active prober ~ the watcher that answers back

''A threat model that assumes a PASSIVE observer under-reads the adversary by a decade.'' The canonical move: watch for a suspicious connection, then ''CONNECT to that endpoint and speak the protocol'' to confirm what the traffic would not reveal. Run at national scale it measured a median half-second from probe to verdict, across sixteen thousand prober addresses of which ninety-five percent appeared exactly once — so blocking the probers bought nothing.

''Any surface that answers a stranger distinguishably reads as an ORACLE, whatever it protects.'' Two circumvention protocols fell exactly here: they authenticated nobody, so anyone speaking the opening move got a distinguishable answer, and a probe for each arrived within months.

''THREE PROPERTIES THE SURVIVING DESIGN NEEDED, and a bare gate holds none of them'':

# ''The CLIENT proves first.'' The prover authenticates before the responder emits a byte — otherwise the responder itself serves as the oracle, whatever it guards.
# ''SILENCE on failure, never rejection.'' No error, no reset, no distinguishable close, and a randomised drop interval so the TIMING carries no tell either. ''A rejection still confesses that something here understood the question.''
# ''The gate SPLITS — silence rides PER-SHRINE, admission rides PER-VESSEL'' (below). A confederation-wide credential at ADMISSION hands one infiltrated member a universal probe key and restores the oracle with an extra step and a false sense of cover; a per-vessel credential at SILENCE cannot run at all.

''And the cheapest adversary never probes at all.'' A power willing to bear collateral damage blocks everything unrecognised and permits a whitelist. Against default-deny, unobservability buys nothing — only ''resembling permitted traffic'' helps, and that shelter stays revocable by whichever platform it borrows.

!!! The pattern that fits ~ Noise `IK`, and the three that disqualify themselves

''The Noise Protocol Framework (revision 34, 2018-07-11) already carries this shape, and its §7.5 token sequences settle the choice by inspection'':

```
IK:  <- s              XK:  <- s            KX:  -> s
     ...                    ...                  ...
     -> e, es, s, ss        -> e, es             -> e
     <- e, ee, se           <- e, ee             <- e, ee, se, s, es
                            -> s, se
```

The `<- s` above the `...` reads //the initiator already holds the responder's static, out of band// — and THAT pre-knowledge carries the probe resistance, never any pre-shared symmetric key. Lacking the responder's static public key, a stranger cannot compute `es`, so it cannot form a message the responder will answer.

* ''`XK` disqualifies itself by construction.'' The initiator's static arrives in message THREE, so the responder must answer a party it has not yet identified — the oracle, restored inside the pattern.
* ''`KX` and `XX` fail from the other side'': no pre-shared responder static, so any stranger opens and draws a reply.
* ''`KK` over-delivers'' — it wants the responder already holding the guest's static, which a host→guest invite alone never establishes.
* ''`IK` fits.'' The guest's static rides ENCRYPTED in message one (`e, es, s, ss`); the responder decrypts it, looks the peer up, and decides whether to answer. Guest speaks first, guest proves first, responder accepts or stays silent.

''The cost `IK` charges, priced here rather than discovered later.'' Noise §7.8 rates `IK` 4/3 on identity hiding and names the initiator's static //"Encrypted to responder's static public key, without forward secrecy. If an attacker learns the responder's private key they can decrypt the initiator's public key."// A seized shrine key therefore ''retroactively de-anonymises every past enrollment message'' an adversary recorded — a leak no refresh cadence (#/cadence) reaches, since the tape already ran.

!!! The split ~ silence per-SHRINE, admission per-VESSEL

''A per-vessel secret CANNOT gate silence, and the arithmetic says why.'' To check a per-vessel credential the responder must first know WHICH vessel — which wants either a cleartext selector on the wire (a stable, linkable per-vessel identifier, precisely what the veil hides) or trial-verification against every enrolled vessel (an O(N) asymmetric-work amplifier handing the adversary a cheaper denial-of-service than the one #cadence defends).

''So the field seats the silence gate on the RESPONDER's key.'' WireGuard §5.3 states the mechanism plainly: //"in order for the responder to remain silent, even while under load, all messages have a first MAC (msg.mac1) that uses the responder's public key. This means that at the very least, a peer sending a message must know to whom it is talking (by virtue of knowing its public key), in order to elicit any kind of response."// That check runs keyed-BLAKE2s ''before any Curve25519 work and before any state gets allocated'' — §5.1 names the goal, //"to avoid storing any state prior to authentication and to not send any responses to unauthenticated packets"//. obfs4 distributes the same shape through its bridge line.

<<~moves per-vessel-secret/gates-silence -> per-shrine-mac/gates-silence on/the-responder-static-key if/pre-asymmetric-stateless-drop do/move-per-vessel-DOWN-to-admission>>

''Per-vessel belongs at ADMISSION'' — the decrypted initiator static out of `IK` message one, looked up in the enrollment table, an unknown static dropped with no reply and no state. Two gates carrying two keys and two jobs: the shrine key buys ''silence toward strangers''; the enrollment record buys ''who may enter''. Fusing them loses both.

''And the `mac1` seat leaks, named here so nobody buys it twice.'' WireGuard §5.3: //"this first MAC allows a passive attacker to make guesses about for which public key the packet is intended, slightly weakening identity hiding properties, though a correct guess would not constitute cryptographic proof since no private material was used in generating the MAC."// A censor holding a list of suspected shrine keys ''confirms traffic bound for one, passively, with no probe at all'' — the exact price of buying silence.

!!! What the pre-shared key buys, and what it never buys

''`psk2` buys a harvest-now-decrypt-later hedge. It buys NO admission.'' Noise §9.4 places the `psk` token by position: `psk0` prepends it to message one; `psk1`, `psk2` append it to the end of message one, message two. WireGuard's construction reads `Noise_IKpsk2_25519_ChaChaPoly_BLAKE2s` — the key mixes in at message TWO, so it ''cannot gate whether the responder answers message one''. §5.2 states the motive directly: //"The attack model here is that adversaries may be recording encrypted traffic on a long term basis, in hopes of someday being able to break Curve25519 and decrypt past traffic"//, and adds that where the pre-shared key leaks //"the Curve25519 keys still provide more than sufficient protection."//

`psk0` WOULD gate message one, and pays the O(N) trial-decryption named above — which only a Household-tier peer count affords. Crossroads and Temple tiers (#/per-tier) cannot.

!!! What the shape still owes

* ''Key confirmation.'' WireGuard §5 holds the responder from sending transport data until the initiator's first encrypted message lands — //"This ordering restriction is to require confirmation as described for KEA+C"//. A handshake skipping it lets a responder commit to a session the peer never confirmed.
* ''A replay window'', which wants a per-peer monotonic counter rather than a clock — the transport form of the no-global-now law, at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands#/two-clocks">>.
* ''Channel binding onto the handshake hash.'' Noise §14 seats it on `h`, never the chaining key `ck`, //"to prevent multiple sessions deriving identical keys through invalid public keys"//. Any vessel-or-persona binding this stack hangs on a session hangs on `h`.
* ''Revocation after an invite leaks'' — OPEN, carried at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/gather-the-fleet">>.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-wire-budget>>

!! The wire carries a BUDGET ~ and the human spends it

''Unobservability names a budget a design SPENDS, never a property it holds.'' Every byte of shape removed costs throughput, latency, or the ability to add a field later — so a design that never declares its budget discovers it under an adversary instead.

|! what it buys |! what it costs |
| a fixed-size first message | stealth against passive classification | ''every future field forks the mesh'' unless the space rides reserved now |
| constant-rate cover traffic | unlinkability of propagation | ''convergence latency, one for one'' |
| a spoken secret rather than a scanned one | unphotographability, and no artifact on any device | a support call when it fails, and no diagnostic payload |
| silence on failure | no probe oracle | a legitimate party sees exactly what an attacker sees |

''AND THE FIXED FIRST MESSAGE BUYS SOMETHING NOBODY ASKED FOR.'' It makes optionality at the handshake ''unexpressible'' — which cures by construction the decay that killed an earlier federation, where optional capabilities pulled the effective feature set down to the intersection and the intersection kept shrinking. A Nexus defined as parsing IDENTICALLY stands on that.

> ''RULED (operator): the HUMAN chooses what the mesh observes.'' This stack does not achieve unobservability and MUST NOT claim to. It offers a dial and prices each setting HONESTLY — the protocol states what a setting actually reveals and actually costs, and the surface names it without a comfortable word standing in for a weaker property. ''The budget belongs to the party who bears the risk''; the labelling belongs to us.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-invite-secret>>

!! The secret rides the VOICE ~ the descriptor rides the code

''One invite, two channels, and an adversary must hold both.''

* ''The scanned code carries the PUBLIC DESCRIPTOR'' — expiry, chant epoch, protocol version, the shrine's key fingerprint, the expected release fingerprint. None of it confidential, all of it checkable ''before a packet leaves'', and loud on mismatch.
* ''The human carries the SECRET'' — short enough to speak or write, and therefore unphotographable from across a room. A scanned bearer token falls to any camera with line of sight and persists in a photo roll; a spoken code leaves ''no artifact on any device''.

''The exchange runs BALANCED, because peers hold no servers.'' An augmented password exchange stores a verifier on one side, and that asymmetry ''IS a server'' however well it performs. A balanced exchange holds the same secret at both ends, assigns no roles, stores nothing, and reads no clock.

''And the password CONSUMES ITSELF.'' It authorises one pairing; the vessels hold static keys afterward and the secret stops existing. So its lifetime reads as ONE USE rather than one hour — a bound that needs no global now to enforce.

''This closes the scanning polarity.'' An adversary presenting a code hands a guest a ''wrong descriptor'', which reads loud, and without the spoken half they cannot complete regardless.

''An open seat, named rather than filled'': a gesture-carried secret would serve a party who cannot speak or hear, and reads as a chosen secret rather than a biometric — revocable, which biometrics never manage. ''But motion carries farther than voice and records without sound'', so the threat model may run opposite to the intuition. Accessibility earns the seat; unobservability does not.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/repair-kit>>

!! The Repair-Kit ~ the delegate's verbs

The maintainer (the bounded repair hand of [[steward-doctrine|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/steward-doctrine]]) carries a small, typed verb-set — each verb a known protocol, none invented here:

| verb | what it does | carried by |
|---|---|---|
| ''re-key'' | rotate a member's key forward; heal a past compromise | TreeKEM / MLS Update — post-compromise security |
| ''re-share'' | re-randomize the threshold shares; expire old theft | PSS refresh (#/reset-the-clock) |
| ''evict'' | blank a member's path so it loses ALL future access | TreeKEM blank-the-path + a [[kapae|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae]] on the membership stack |
| ''heal-partition'' | re-converge two halves after a split | gossip / epidemic anti-entropy |
| ''recover-from-fork'' | re-anchor a forked island to a trusted line | weak-subjectivity CHECKPOINT — a guardian-signed re-genesis seed |
| ''re-genesis'' | re-seed a destroyed island from the guardians | guardian-threshold social-recovery + a TIME-LOCK |

Two verbs carry a sharp edge worth naming. ''evict'' runs forward-only — TreeKEM's blank-the-path removes //future// access (the evicted member keeps what it already read); pair it with the membership-stack [[kapae|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae]] so the eviction shadows resurrection. ''re-genesis'' carries a ''TIME-LOCK by design'' — a guardian-threshold can re-seed a lost island, but the time-lock gives the legitimate operator a veto window to refuse an //attacker's// recovery attempt. Without the lock, the social-recovery path itself becomes the adversary's cleanest entry — designation must carry authority and fail loud, never recover silently.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nothing-reconstructs>>

!! Two recovery shapes ~ and only one lets a secret exist again

''This stack holds two, and they diverge exactly where it matters.'' A SPLIT SEED reassembles at recovery — a transient window in which the whole secret stands somewhere. A THRESHOLD ATTESTATION gives each guardian their own key, has each SIGN the rotation independently, and ''assembles nothing''; the recovered authority rotates a fresh key rather than resurrecting an old one.

> ''The second reads strictly better, and the stack already built it.'' So the live question never asks what shape arrived most recently — it asks ''whether the split seed needs to materialise at all''. Where it does not, the split retires, and no secret in the stack ever exists twice.

''And a shard belongs off a screen.'' A shard carried in a scanned code lands in a photo roll, which lands on a synced device, which lands in a backup. Paper's whole virtue reads: ''it does not sync.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/self-stabilization>>

!! Self-Stabilization ~ the FLOW self-heal + its biological shape

The FLOW clock's self-heal carries ''Dijkstra self-stabilization'' — a system that converges to a correct state from ANY corrupted starting state, with no external reset. The necrospire may corrupt a replica arbitrarily; the honest gossip neighbourhood drags it back to correct without a maintainer touching it. This property does the heavy lifting under siege: it means the mesh tolerates //transient// corruption everywhere and still re-converges, so the maintainer's repair-kit handles only //persistent// capture.

The biological shape carries real structure, never decoration:

* ''artificial immune systems'' — decentralized defence with no king, immune //memory// of past intrusions, and distributed detection. The mesh defends the way an organism does: many local detectors, no central guard to fall.
* ''Stafford Beer's Viable System Model (VSM)'' — recursive autonomy maps the Lararium → Nexus → DreamNet ladder slot-for-slot: each tier runs a viable system //containing// viable systems. The ''algedonic signal'' — VSM's pain-alert that bypasses the normal channels to escalate fast — carries the IDS alarm straight up the tiers, shortening the refresh window (#/cadence) without waiting on routine reporting.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/per-tier>>

!! Per-Tier ~ run the whole kit at every shrine

The two clocks, the cadence, and the repair-kit run ''at each shrine tier autonomously'' — Household, Crossroads, Temple — each tier a viable system that refreshes its own authority and self-heals its own flow without a parent's permission (VSM recursion, #self-stabilization). No tier waits on the tier above to survive a local breach.

This autonomy explains a build decision already taken: the Herm (the crossroads-marker, the minimal wayfarer of [[vessel-caps|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps]]) now carries the ''@daemon'' as its keel — so even a pure-carriage waystation runs its own refresh clock and repair hand, rather than depending on a distant operator to defend it. A mesh whose smallest nodes can each reset their own clock has no single window the necrospire can pry.

<<~/ahu>>

